00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:23.820
So for those of you who are
familiar with analytics,

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:28.590
this is a very basic
analytics flow overview.

00:03:28.590 --> 00:03:30.210
This is probably
really familiar.

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:33.870
There's four stages to every
analytics flow, ingest,

00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:36.540
storage, analysis,
and visualization.

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:41.610
Starting on the left
of there, your game

00:03:41.610 --> 00:03:43.110
is where you're
actually going to be

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:47.310
generating those analytic
events, your telemetry events.

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:49.410
You'll ingest them
generally through a gateway.

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:52.290
We have a lot of customers that
just do those using an HTTP

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:53.970
REST Endpoint.

00:03:53.970 --> 00:03:57.940
Some of them like to use, for
example, App Engine, I think.

00:03:57.940 --> 00:03:59.940
Later on we're going to
hear from a customer who

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:01.970
does it using Container Engine.

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:08.730
Storage obviously, we have a
really robust object storage

00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:11.460
available, and we
actually recommend

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:13.320
that you go ahead and
store all of your data

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:14.250
directly in BigQuery.

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:17.970
And I'll discuss that in
more detail in a minute.

00:04:17.970 --> 00:04:19.429
And then the analysis
portion where

00:04:19.429 --> 00:04:21.969
you're going to have your data
scientists or engineers figure

00:04:21.969 --> 00:04:23.580
out what the insights
are and build

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:25.590
some visualizations
that you can then

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:27.330
give to the development team.

00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:29.400
That's mostly going to
be covered by Daniel

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:31.680
a little later.

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:33.390
This is the flow
from left to right.

00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:36.300
Ideally, what we wanted
to get to is the flow back

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:38.490
from your dev team
taking those insights,

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:42.180
putting them into your game
to increase engagement,

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:43.890
make players happier
with the things

00:04:43.890 --> 00:04:46.230
that you're doing in the
game, help you monetize.

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:55.320
This is a reference architecture
for Google Cloud Platform Game

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:55.920
Analytics.

00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:59.040
So real-time and batch
are both supported.

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:00.480
For example, your
real-time events

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:03.600
may be coming from devices
or your game client.

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:05.340
Batch loading,
it's really common

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:08.580
to have folks send batches
of analytics events

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:11.010
after a match is finished
or directly from the server

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:13.500
that people are playing on.

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:15.420
For the ingest
portion of that, we

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:20.550
recommend that you use Google
Cloud Pub/Sub to take real-time

00:05:20.550 --> 00:05:22.710
And if you're going
to do a batch ingest,

00:05:22.710 --> 00:05:26.550
you can just upload the file
directly into Cloud Storage.

00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:33.120
And we have an ETL service
that we call Cloud Dataflow.

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:36.120
It's capable of taking your
events both from the real-time

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:38.880
and the batch segments
and putting them

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:40.002
into BigQuery for you.

00:05:40.002 --> 00:05:41.460
You can also
transform them if they

00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:43.920
need to be in a different
format for the way you

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:48.000
want to store them in your data
warehouse or your data lake.

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:52.147
We do offer a data exploration
tool called Cloud Datalab.

00:05:52.147 --> 00:05:54.480
We'll be going over that a
little bit later in the Talk.

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:57.810
That's really useful for your
analysts and your scientists

00:05:57.810 --> 00:06:00.030
to quickly explore
the data, figure out

00:06:00.030 --> 00:06:02.552
where the insights are, use
that to build the reports

00:06:02.552 --> 00:06:05.010
that you're going to send to
your dev team or your business

00:06:05.010 --> 00:06:06.810
analyst people.

00:06:06.810 --> 00:06:08.520
This is the reference
architecture.

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:10.650
If you want to get into
that in more detail,

00:06:10.650 --> 00:06:12.900
we do have installations
around the show called

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:14.640
Tree House where
you can look at this

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:18.110
and dig in a little bit on it.

00:06:18.110 --> 00:06:20.120
The first part is the ingest.

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:26.270
As I mentioned before, your
first section of the ingest

00:06:26.270 --> 00:06:29.780
is almost always going
to be either pulling it

00:06:29.780 --> 00:06:36.560
in via real-time in Pub/Sub or
batch in Google Cloud Storage.

00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:39.920
So the advantage of going
with a real-time flow, which

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:43.940
you can see here uses Pub/Sub
on the left, into data flow,

00:06:43.940 --> 00:06:46.609
and then inserts from
that into BigQuery.

00:06:46.609 --> 00:06:47.900
So you get very little latency.

00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:49.400
We do have gaming
customers that are

00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:53.360
able to achieve single
second latency on analytics

00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:56.390
using this exact workflow.

00:06:56.390 --> 00:06:58.400
You do get guaranteed
delivery with anything

00:06:58.400 --> 00:06:59.720
that you put into Pub/Sub.

00:06:59.720 --> 00:07:02.240
Once you've sent it to Pub/Sub
and Pub/Sub acknowledges it,

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:06.050
it's guaranteed that we're
going to deliver that message.

00:07:06.050 --> 00:07:11.300
You can put extract transform
and load operations, ETL stuff,

00:07:11.300 --> 00:07:13.280
in data flow if you need it.

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:16.069
If you don't want to
do any transforming,

00:07:16.069 --> 00:07:18.110
you just want to use that
as a data runner to get

00:07:18.110 --> 00:07:24.170
the real-time stuff from the
Pub/Sub pipeline into BigQuery,

00:07:24.170 --> 00:07:27.039
you can also just issue
the ETL completely.

00:07:27.039 --> 00:07:28.580
These are all fully
managed services,

00:07:28.580 --> 00:07:30.110
so you don't have
to choose nodes,

00:07:30.110 --> 00:07:31.927
you don't have to choose tiers.

00:07:31.927 --> 00:07:33.010
There's nothing like that.

00:07:33.010 --> 00:07:38.250
It scales to handle however
many requests you send to it.

00:07:38.250 --> 00:07:41.400
On the batch side this
is as simple as it gets.

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:44.250
You stick a file in
Google Cloud Storage,

00:07:44.250 --> 00:07:45.990
you load the file into BigQuery.

00:07:45.990 --> 00:07:49.770
There's very little cost
here, very little complexity.

00:07:49.770 --> 00:07:52.050
It's asynchronous, so
you can do it whenever

00:07:52.050 --> 00:07:53.560
it's convenient for you.

00:07:53.560 --> 00:07:54.870
This is really low friction.

00:07:54.870 --> 00:08:00.240
If you don't already have a lot
of analytics event generating

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:04.020
code in your client yet, you can
start writing events directly

00:08:04.020 --> 00:08:07.092
to a local log on whatever
the user's machine is,

00:08:07.092 --> 00:08:09.300
and then just upload that
log to Google Cloud Storage

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:11.700
at the end of their session.

00:08:11.700 --> 00:08:14.472
Dataflow ETL can be put
in between these two--

00:08:14.472 --> 00:08:15.930
like we saw in the
previous slide--

00:08:15.930 --> 00:08:19.400
if you need to
transform the data.

00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:21.800
Actually storing the
data, the two big options

00:08:21.800 --> 00:08:26.660
are Cloud Storage, and the
advantage of Cloud Storage

00:08:26.660 --> 00:08:28.400
really is just how
easy it is to use

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:30.260
and how cost effective it is.

00:08:30.260 --> 00:08:32.150
We do have multiple
storage classes.

00:08:32.150 --> 00:08:33.983
If the data that you're
putting there you're

00:08:33.983 --> 00:08:35.690
not expecting to
touch anytime soon,

00:08:35.690 --> 00:08:38.510
you can choose one of
the storage classes

00:08:38.510 --> 00:08:40.820
that is lower cost.

00:08:40.820 --> 00:08:43.589
It's really easy to
integrate uploading

00:08:43.589 --> 00:08:45.380
to Google Cloud Storage
with whatever tools

00:08:45.380 --> 00:08:46.490
you're using now.

00:08:46.490 --> 00:08:50.876
If you're using another
public clouds object storage,

00:08:50.876 --> 00:08:52.250
for example,
chances are the tool

00:08:52.250 --> 00:08:54.800
that you're using to
upload to that will also

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:57.020
upload to Google Cloud Storage.

00:08:57.020 --> 00:09:01.310
If you want to set a time
to live, like an expiration,

00:09:01.310 --> 00:09:03.420
whenever you upload
you're allowed to do that.

00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:05.030
This is great if you
want to take away

00:09:05.030 --> 00:09:06.710
some of the headache
of managing,

00:09:06.710 --> 00:09:10.910
or having an admin team check
your objects every month

00:09:10.910 --> 00:09:12.320
to see what needs to be deleted.

00:09:12.320 --> 00:09:15.140
To keep the costs in
check, you can just

00:09:15.140 --> 00:09:17.630
set a time that you want it
to be automatically deleted

00:09:17.630 --> 00:09:18.920
whenever you upload it.

00:09:18.920 --> 00:09:21.230
And obviously,
Google Cloud Storage

00:09:21.230 --> 00:09:26.090
is a first class citizen across
the Google Cloud platform,

00:09:26.090 --> 00:09:30.270
so all of our products hook
into it very seamlessly.

00:09:30.270 --> 00:09:32.000
It's really easy to use.

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:34.610
For example, if you want
to upload from Google Cloud

00:09:34.610 --> 00:09:37.370
Storage into BigQuery-- that
batch pipeline I was showing

00:09:37.370 --> 00:09:39.050
earlier that was very simple--

00:09:39.050 --> 00:09:42.320
this is how simple the
dialog box for doing that is.

00:09:42.320 --> 00:09:45.885
All you do is choose Google
Cloud Storage, put in the URL,

00:09:45.885 --> 00:09:49.430
tell it where you want
it to put the data--

00:09:49.430 --> 00:09:50.780
like the name of the table--

00:09:50.780 --> 00:09:54.050
and whether you want it to
append or overwrite that table,

00:09:54.050 --> 00:09:56.860
or only write if there's
nothing in the table yet.

00:09:56.860 --> 00:09:58.820
And you can have it just
automatically detect

00:09:58.820 --> 00:10:01.130
your schema-- you can also
specify that if you like--

00:10:01.130 --> 00:10:02.160
but it's super simple.

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:07.710
The other storage option,
and the one that I prefer,

00:10:07.710 --> 00:10:09.370
and that I would
recommend you to do,

00:10:09.370 --> 00:10:12.420
is just go ahead and store
everything in BigQuery.

00:10:12.420 --> 00:10:18.750
The reason why primarily
is because the actual cost

00:10:18.750 --> 00:10:19.920
is identical.

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:25.050
The Cloud Storage
standard cost here has

00:10:25.050 --> 00:10:28.440
the same price per gigabyte as
just storing the data directly

00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:30.330
in BigQuery.

00:10:30.330 --> 00:10:32.490
It has the same TTL
support as what you

00:10:32.490 --> 00:10:34.800
have in Google Cloud Storage.

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:37.350
A lot of the visualization
tools that you folks are already

00:10:37.350 --> 00:10:39.766
going to be using, and that
are really popular in the game

00:10:39.766 --> 00:10:42.570
industry support
BigQuery naively.

00:10:42.570 --> 00:10:45.600
And you can directly
upload to it or stream

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:47.610
to it if you choose to do that.

00:10:47.610 --> 00:10:50.310
Also if you don't touch
a table for 90 days,

00:10:50.310 --> 00:10:51.601
we cut the price in half.

00:10:51.601 --> 00:10:53.100
We announced that
last year at Next.

00:10:53.100 --> 00:10:54.550
We're really excited about that.

00:10:54.550 --> 00:10:56.383
A lot of customers
really like that feature.

00:10:58.627 --> 00:11:00.710
Now if I can switch over
to the demo real quickly.

00:11:03.950 --> 00:11:06.106
I'm briefly going
to go over actually

00:11:06.106 --> 00:11:08.480
what it looks like when you're
querying the data that you

00:11:08.480 --> 00:11:10.040
have in BigQuery.

00:11:10.040 --> 00:11:13.340
This is mostly just to show
you the scale and the speed

00:11:13.340 --> 00:11:16.340
that this operates at
is really impressive.

00:11:16.340 --> 00:11:19.580
We get new customers every
day that are amazed by this.

00:11:19.580 --> 00:11:22.130
So here we have some
manufacturer data.

00:11:22.130 --> 00:11:25.370
Shockingly-- although we did
talk to some game companies

00:11:25.370 --> 00:11:27.590
about it-- no one was
super excited to give us

00:11:27.590 --> 00:11:29.720
a bunch of their
production game data

00:11:29.720 --> 00:11:33.320
to show to you folks today,
so we manufactured some data

00:11:33.320 --> 00:11:37.850
so that we could show you
the speed of BigQuery.

00:11:37.850 --> 00:11:40.250
You can see these
are pretty typical

00:11:40.250 --> 00:11:42.890
event telemetry
analytics tables.

00:11:42.890 --> 00:11:44.305
Some of these are pretty large.

00:11:44.305 --> 00:11:45.680
Here's what a
preview of it looks

00:11:45.680 --> 00:11:48.320
like where it's going to have
the user and the time stamp,

00:11:48.320 --> 00:11:50.780
and the time that they did
something, what their character

00:11:50.780 --> 00:11:53.820
stats looked like when
they generated that event.

00:11:53.820 --> 00:11:56.330
So you can see that
this particular table

00:11:56.330 --> 00:12:01.760
has 98 gigabytes and
about half a billion rows.

00:12:01.760 --> 00:12:03.740
We have another table here.

00:12:03.740 --> 00:12:05.600
It's more of the
same kind of data--

00:12:05.600 --> 00:12:12.110
244 gigabytes and
almost 1.3 billion rows.

00:12:12.110 --> 00:12:14.030
Yesterday as I was
playing around with this--

00:12:14.030 --> 00:12:17.420
trying to come up
with a demo query

00:12:17.420 --> 00:12:20.330
that I liked that was
sort of impressive--

00:12:20.330 --> 00:12:24.800
I ran this one kind of to
see if I could figure out

00:12:24.800 --> 00:12:27.680
what the limitations of
the manufactured data were.

00:12:27.680 --> 00:12:30.620
This might be somewhat amusing.

00:12:30.620 --> 00:12:34.490
This particular one-- if we want
to open this up a little bit--

00:12:34.490 --> 00:12:40.440
we can see here that it queried
20 gigabytes in 2.4 seconds.

00:12:40.440 --> 00:12:42.430
This is a pretty
complicated query.

00:12:42.430 --> 00:12:43.670
It has two sub queries.

00:12:43.670 --> 00:12:45.020
It's doing some aggregation.

00:12:45.020 --> 00:12:48.920
It's doing three different
levels of regular expression

00:12:48.920 --> 00:12:50.180
extractions.

00:12:50.180 --> 00:12:53.480
So what this has is the
number of times people

00:12:53.480 --> 00:12:55.550
started a level, the
number of times people

00:12:55.550 --> 00:12:58.090
completed a level, and then
the completion percentage,

00:12:58.090 --> 00:12:59.720
which is obviously
just calculated

00:12:59.720 --> 00:13:00.685
from those two numbers.

00:13:00.685 --> 00:13:02.060
This gives you a
pretty good idea

00:13:02.060 --> 00:13:04.380
of what the person who
manufactured this data put in

00:13:04.380 --> 00:13:09.110
as what they thought the
percentage of stages completion

00:13:09.110 --> 00:13:11.150
should be is 52%, clearly.

00:13:11.150 --> 00:13:14.930
And in stage 10, they'd had
it so that people are only

00:13:14.930 --> 00:13:16.640
completing 23% of
the time so that it

00:13:16.640 --> 00:13:20.010
would be good demo data.

00:13:20.010 --> 00:13:24.740
Again, this query speed
is really the point

00:13:24.740 --> 00:13:28.280
that I'm trying to
drive home here.

00:13:28.280 --> 00:13:31.490
I have tried to
write a query that

00:13:31.490 --> 00:13:33.890
would touch as much data as
possible in this data set,

00:13:33.890 --> 00:13:38.360
and I could not generate a query
that took more than 60 seconds.

00:13:38.360 --> 00:13:42.729
So hopefully that's
reasonably fast.

00:13:42.729 --> 00:13:44.520
Yeah, if we can save
questions for the end.

00:13:44.520 --> 00:13:46.150
Hopefully that's
reasonably fast.

00:13:46.150 --> 00:13:48.350
Show you this is a
very powerful tool.

00:13:48.350 --> 00:13:50.380
Now I'm going to have
our customer come up.

00:13:50.380 --> 00:13:51.610
They're going to talk
about how they actually

00:13:51.610 --> 00:13:53.680
use these tools in
production environments.

00:13:53.680 --> 00:13:55.513
If we can switch back
to the slides, please?

00:13:58.288 --> 00:14:00.246
MITJA BEZENSEK: Thank
you for the introduction.

00:14:04.530 --> 00:14:07.620
Today I'm going to be
presenting with my friend,

00:14:07.620 --> 00:14:09.600
Talking Tom here.

00:14:09.600 --> 00:14:15.150
What do we do at Outfit7
regarding the analytics,

00:14:15.150 --> 00:14:21.120
how does our pipeline look
like, what data do we use,

00:14:21.120 --> 00:14:24.570
and why do we use it for?

00:14:24.570 --> 00:14:28.080
So Talking Tom
and Friends is one

00:14:28.080 --> 00:14:32.370
of the most recognized mobile
franchises around the world

00:14:32.370 --> 00:14:35.880
and is the top runner on
all mobile app stores.

00:14:35.880 --> 00:14:40.330
That includes your Google
Play Stores, Apple App Stores,

00:14:40.330 --> 00:14:44.910
Amazon, also Microsoft
or Windows Apps Stores.

00:14:44.910 --> 00:14:47.310
Now, how it all started?

00:14:47.310 --> 00:14:52.350
It started in 2010 when
we released our first app.

00:14:52.350 --> 00:14:54.480
It was simply
called Talking Tom,

00:14:54.480 --> 00:14:56.070
and you can still download it.

00:14:56.070 --> 00:14:58.380
It's a really fun
app to play with.

00:14:58.380 --> 00:14:59.340
So you've got a cat.

00:14:59.340 --> 00:15:01.590
You pet the cat.

00:15:01.590 --> 00:15:04.740
You can talk to the
cat, and it talks back

00:15:04.740 --> 00:15:06.090
in a really funny voice.

00:15:06.090 --> 00:15:09.600
So it went really
viral at the time.

00:15:09.600 --> 00:15:12.630
We didn't just sit
back and relax.

00:15:12.630 --> 00:15:15.300
The next year, we
released a new game

00:15:15.300 --> 00:15:18.210
called Simply
Talking Tom 2, which

00:15:18.210 --> 00:15:23.140
enabled the player or the
user to customize Tom.

00:15:23.140 --> 00:15:26.430
So you could buy a
fancy wardrobe for him,

00:15:26.430 --> 00:15:31.080
like this really cool hat that
you see, or give him a toy.

00:15:31.080 --> 00:15:34.020
Now, really for the
gaming analytics,

00:15:34.020 --> 00:15:39.270
it started going on or
happening in 2013 when

00:15:39.270 --> 00:15:41.220
we released our first game.

00:15:41.220 --> 00:15:43.770
So before those were
more apps, things

00:15:43.770 --> 00:15:46.710
like you would take
out of your pocket,

00:15:46.710 --> 00:15:50.490
and play with for one
minute, and then put it back

00:15:50.490 --> 00:15:51.630
in your pocket.

00:15:51.630 --> 00:15:55.920
Now with My Talking
Tom released in 2013,

00:15:55.920 --> 00:15:58.590
this was the first
fully fledged Tamagotchi

00:15:58.590 --> 00:16:03.450
game, where you take care
of your cat, you feed him.

00:16:07.830 --> 00:16:10.500
He has to take a shower
every now and then.

00:16:10.500 --> 00:16:14.070
And if he's hungry,
he tells you.

00:16:14.070 --> 00:16:17.790
And with that the
gaming analytics

00:16:17.790 --> 00:16:22.740
become much more important and
much more fun to deal with.

00:16:22.740 --> 00:16:28.470
Because with games you have
stuff like in-app purchases,

00:16:28.470 --> 00:16:31.560
you have more
space, how to target

00:16:31.560 --> 00:16:39.940
different ads, and all sort
of ways to do monetization.

00:16:39.940 --> 00:16:41.250
But we didn't stop there.

00:16:41.250 --> 00:16:43.770
During the years
we added more games

00:16:43.770 --> 00:16:47.260
to the portfolio, more
talking characters.

00:16:47.260 --> 00:16:50.790
And also different games
like an endless runner

00:16:50.790 --> 00:16:55.650
called Talking Tom Goldrun
and a real-time multiplayer

00:16:55.650 --> 00:16:58.810
game called Talking
Tom Bubble Shooter.

00:16:58.810 --> 00:17:01.440
So the things got
more and more complex,

00:17:01.440 --> 00:17:06.240
and also there was more
and more data to process.

00:17:06.240 --> 00:17:08.910
What does this mean
in terms of numbers?

00:17:08.910 --> 00:17:15.030
So we have almost six
billion downloads,

00:17:15.030 --> 00:17:18.119
and this is still
growing till this day.

00:17:18.119 --> 00:17:24.660
And 348 million users playing
our games each and every day.

00:17:24.660 --> 00:17:29.130
So you can imagine how
much data we can collect

00:17:29.130 --> 00:17:32.265
from all of those users.

00:17:32.265 --> 00:17:34.920
So this is a number--

00:17:34.920 --> 00:17:37.320
each and every day
a rough estimate

00:17:37.320 --> 00:17:43.560
is we stream five billion
BigQuery rows per day.

00:17:43.560 --> 00:17:46.290
So our processing
pipeline handles

00:17:46.290 --> 00:17:53.400
from 30,000 BigQuery events
per second to even 100,000.

00:17:53.400 --> 00:17:56.880
It depends on the
time of the day.

00:17:56.880 --> 00:18:02.480
So this is different data, like
in-app purchase, advertising

00:18:02.480 --> 00:18:04.620
data, what the user clicked.

00:18:04.620 --> 00:18:07.140
So all different
types of events.

00:18:10.480 --> 00:18:13.940
Now, how does it all happen?

00:18:13.940 --> 00:18:19.610
What you see on the screen is
our data ingestion pipeline.

00:18:19.610 --> 00:18:22.970
This uses all the components
that the guys before me

00:18:22.970 --> 00:18:27.740
explained, like Google
Cloud Pub/Sub, Dataflow.

00:18:31.490 --> 00:18:34.160
Before BigQuery, we were
doing game analytics,

00:18:34.160 --> 00:18:36.620
but we did it in a
really primitive way.

00:18:36.620 --> 00:18:41.420
We had some sharded counters
in Google Cloud Data Store.

00:18:41.420 --> 00:18:46.090
We used to parse logs and
dump them into SQL databases,

00:18:46.090 --> 00:18:48.260
so that really didn't scale.

00:18:48.260 --> 00:18:53.720
Also we tried using Google
Analytics and again,

00:18:53.720 --> 00:18:57.860
that didn't scale at our pace
and didn't provide the features

00:18:57.860 --> 00:18:58.700
that we need.

00:18:58.700 --> 00:19:02.690
So this is what we
came up with then.

00:19:02.690 --> 00:19:05.660
When BigQuery came out,
this was a real lifesaver

00:19:05.660 --> 00:19:08.600
for our Game Analytics.

00:19:08.600 --> 00:19:16.970
On the screen-- if we go
from the right to the left--

00:19:16.970 --> 00:19:20.090
mobile client sent
some events, and this

00:19:20.090 --> 00:19:25.430
is received by REST API Endpoint
running on Google Container

00:19:25.430 --> 00:19:26.420
Engine.

00:19:26.420 --> 00:19:28.580
So that does some
basic validation

00:19:28.580 --> 00:19:30.770
and stores the event
in Google Cloud

00:19:30.770 --> 00:19:34.440
Pub/Sub for later processing.

00:19:34.440 --> 00:19:39.780
And then we have a
Dataflow job on your left.

00:19:39.780 --> 00:19:44.570
The Dataflow job takes the event
from the Google Cloud Pub/Sub,

00:19:44.570 --> 00:19:46.640
does some extended
validation, does

00:19:46.640 --> 00:19:49.970
some enrichment of the
events before writing those

00:19:49.970 --> 00:19:52.100
into BigQuery.

00:19:52.100 --> 00:19:56.780
And then in BigQuery, we
have some business analytics

00:19:56.780 --> 00:19:59.240
to analyze that
data, and also we

00:19:59.240 --> 00:20:05.980
have some data scientists
that also explore

00:20:05.980 --> 00:20:09.050
all the data that we gathered.

00:20:09.050 --> 00:20:12.260
But it's not just the client
events that we are collecting,

00:20:12.260 --> 00:20:15.950
we are also collecting events
from our mobile back-end.

00:20:15.950 --> 00:20:19.730
We have a back-end also
running from Google App Engine,

00:20:19.730 --> 00:20:23.880
and we're generating events
on Google App Engine,

00:20:23.880 --> 00:20:27.200
and sending them
into pull queues.

00:20:27.200 --> 00:20:30.800
And then again a
Dataflow processing job

00:20:30.800 --> 00:20:32.720
takes those events
from the pull queues

00:20:32.720 --> 00:20:35.120
before inserting
them to BigQuery.

00:20:35.120 --> 00:20:40.210
And again, they are available
for further analysis.

00:20:40.210 --> 00:20:41.980
Why do we use this pipeline?

00:20:41.980 --> 00:20:46.130
From a technical standpoint,
it's a really good pipeline.

00:20:46.130 --> 00:20:48.370
I can tell you that from
my personal experience,

00:20:48.370 --> 00:20:52.270
because I work with
this every day.

00:20:52.270 --> 00:20:54.940
One thing is flexibility.

00:20:54.940 --> 00:20:58.030
You may think there are a
lot of blocks just to get

00:20:58.030 --> 00:21:00.280
the data inside the BigQuery.

00:21:00.280 --> 00:21:03.010
But really this is very
flexible because we

00:21:03.010 --> 00:21:07.240
can do upgrades for
individual components

00:21:07.240 --> 00:21:09.530
and not affect the
other components.

00:21:09.530 --> 00:21:13.340
So this really makes an
effective pipeline for us,

00:21:13.340 --> 00:21:16.810
and also we can scale each and
every component separately.

00:21:16.810 --> 00:21:21.390
For example, if
we need to do even

00:21:21.390 --> 00:21:26.290
a stronger enrichment
in the Dataflow job,

00:21:26.290 --> 00:21:27.700
we add more notes.

00:21:27.700 --> 00:21:30.040
So we just scale on
the Dataflow part,

00:21:30.040 --> 00:21:32.610
and we only pay for
that Dataflow part.

00:21:32.610 --> 00:21:36.730
If this was one big box
and we would want to scale,

00:21:36.730 --> 00:21:38.750
we would need to scale
the whole big box.

00:21:38.750 --> 00:21:42.190
So that would cost more money.

00:21:45.050 --> 00:21:49.390
And also the high
availability is really good.

00:21:49.390 --> 00:21:52.630
So for example, if
BigQuery goes down,

00:21:52.630 --> 00:21:55.840
the events are still stored
in Google Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:21:55.840 --> 00:22:01.150
Now, the BigQuery doesn't go
down often, but it can happen.

00:22:01.150 --> 00:22:05.170
This is a very good
design to avoid failures.

00:22:05.170 --> 00:22:08.140
Because you still have events
sitting in the Google Cloud

00:22:08.140 --> 00:22:12.820
Pub/Sub, and when BigQuery
picks back up, then also

00:22:12.820 --> 00:22:15.850
the Dataflow job
restarts and picks

00:22:15.850 --> 00:22:17.890
back up where it left off.

00:22:17.890 --> 00:22:20.170
So really good pipeline.

00:22:20.170 --> 00:22:24.340
And if you're familiar with
microservices way of designing

00:22:24.340 --> 00:22:27.730
things, those are
all the things that

00:22:27.730 --> 00:22:30.150
come under the hood
of mircoservices.

00:22:33.850 --> 00:22:37.870
What do we do with this data?

00:22:37.870 --> 00:22:39.850
I'm going to present some--

00:22:39.850 --> 00:22:42.520
actually two
charts-- what we were

00:22:42.520 --> 00:22:46.430
analyzing, what is our
business benefit from that.

00:22:46.430 --> 00:22:50.500
And the first slide shows a
number of application installs

00:22:50.500 --> 00:22:52.300
during the days.

00:22:52.300 --> 00:22:53.380
There's no surprise.

00:22:53.380 --> 00:22:58.120
You see the blue bar shows
how many installs were there

00:22:58.120 --> 00:23:02.440
in a day, and you see that the
highest bars are usually-- you

00:23:02.440 --> 00:23:04.160
can guess-- those are weekend.

00:23:04.160 --> 00:23:08.170
So more players are engaged
during the weekends.

00:23:08.170 --> 00:23:10.810
And the green line
shows our rank

00:23:10.810 --> 00:23:13.450
for the app on the App Store.

00:23:13.450 --> 00:23:16.160
So these are two charts--

00:23:16.160 --> 00:23:18.400
one is for Talking Tom
Goldrun, and the other

00:23:18.400 --> 00:23:24.340
is for My Talking Tom-- that's
still our flagship game.

00:23:24.340 --> 00:23:28.000
And this is important in
mobile gaming installs

00:23:28.000 --> 00:23:33.130
because it directly
affects our revenue.

00:23:33.130 --> 00:23:36.690
If you want to be
successful in mobile gaming,

00:23:36.690 --> 00:23:39.940
getting new users, getting
installs all the time

00:23:39.940 --> 00:23:41.920
is really key to the success.

00:23:41.920 --> 00:23:47.170
So this is very important
for the business analytics--

00:23:47.170 --> 00:23:49.060
getting more and more installs.

00:23:49.060 --> 00:23:52.840
And also rank on the
app store is important.

00:23:52.840 --> 00:23:57.790
If the rank on the
app store goes down,

00:23:57.790 --> 00:24:02.350
if not so many users
are downloading our app,

00:24:02.350 --> 00:24:05.650
we can arrange a
marketing campaign

00:24:05.650 --> 00:24:09.210
to acquire new players.

00:24:09.210 --> 00:24:13.750
Without this data, this
would be very hard to do.

00:24:13.750 --> 00:24:16.870
And also we have some
machine learning algorithms,

00:24:16.870 --> 00:24:19.990
which decide based on
the number of installs

00:24:19.990 --> 00:24:23.110
by region what kind
of ads to serve.

00:24:25.880 --> 00:24:29.540
The last chart that
I'm going to explain

00:24:29.540 --> 00:24:32.720
is from our multiplayer games.

00:24:32.720 --> 00:24:37.700
So this shows the distribution
of matches in our multiplayer

00:24:37.700 --> 00:24:39.500
games throughout the world.

00:24:39.500 --> 00:24:43.370
We see the darker areas means
that in the last 24 hours

00:24:43.370 --> 00:24:46.250
there were more
players active there.

00:24:46.250 --> 00:24:52.700
And you can see in the
gray stacked charts

00:24:52.700 --> 00:24:57.020
below how many players were
active in a particular part

00:24:57.020 --> 00:24:59.190
of the day in a
specific country.

00:24:59.190 --> 00:25:03.200
So we have Russia,
Brazil, China, and so on.

00:25:03.200 --> 00:25:05.990
We can see how many
users were active.

00:25:05.990 --> 00:25:10.210
But what's important
here, maybe more

00:25:10.210 --> 00:25:13.730
than the number of
matches, is the match time.

00:25:13.730 --> 00:25:17.510
And this is
represented by the line

00:25:17.510 --> 00:25:24.680
that you see over the
number of players chart.

00:25:24.680 --> 00:25:27.620
The matching time
means that if I started

00:25:27.620 --> 00:25:30.140
playing a game against
another player,

00:25:30.140 --> 00:25:34.810
it means how long I have to
wait for the other player

00:25:34.810 --> 00:25:36.480
to start playing against me.

00:25:36.480 --> 00:25:40.730
And if this time is long, it
means that user experience will

00:25:40.730 --> 00:25:44.870
not be that good,
and of course, users

00:25:44.870 --> 00:25:46.790
will not be using our apps.

00:25:46.790 --> 00:25:49.160
So we want to have the
best user experience,

00:25:49.160 --> 00:25:53.120
and we track things like
these matching times.

00:25:53.120 --> 00:25:56.900
And it's not only the
business analysts that

00:25:56.900 --> 00:25:59.180
are looking at these numbers.

00:25:59.180 --> 00:26:02.720
We also have these metrics
pulled from BigQuery

00:26:02.720 --> 00:26:05.900
to Stackdrive, and
our DevOps team

00:26:05.900 --> 00:26:08.240
has some alerts tied
to these metrics,

00:26:08.240 --> 00:26:09.470
like the matching time.

00:26:09.470 --> 00:26:12.780
And if the matching
time is too high,

00:26:12.780 --> 00:26:14.570
then they get some
alerts, and they

00:26:14.570 --> 00:26:17.480
can check whether
the servers are OK

00:26:17.480 --> 00:26:20.120
and if the infrastructure
is running as planned.

00:26:23.290 --> 00:26:28.030
Now with this I will
conclude my part.

00:26:28.030 --> 00:26:41.200
Just to summarize-- we really
think that Google's analytics

00:26:41.200 --> 00:26:45.250
platform is really providing--
is the key to our long term

00:26:45.250 --> 00:26:47.750
mobile success.

00:26:47.750 --> 00:26:50.610
So with that I'm going
to turn over to Daniel.

00:26:50.610 --> 00:26:54.660
He'll explain more about data
exploration and visualization.

00:26:54.660 --> 00:26:55.448
Thank you.

00:26:55.448 --> 00:27:02.497
[APPLAUSE]

00:27:02.497 --> 00:27:04.080
DANIEL GRACHANIN:
Thanks a lot, Mitja.

00:27:06.962 --> 00:27:09.170
As Mitja said, I am going
to go over data exploration

00:27:09.170 --> 00:27:10.347
and visualization.

00:27:10.347 --> 00:27:11.930
This will kind of
cover on your stack,

00:27:11.930 --> 00:27:14.060
although it's not partially
any particular person.

00:27:14.060 --> 00:27:17.750
But typically, this would
be your data scientists,

00:27:17.750 --> 00:27:19.737
data analysts maybe,
your business analysts

00:27:19.737 --> 00:27:21.570
are probably more on
the visualization side.

00:27:21.570 --> 00:27:23.528
But this exploration side
we're going to review

00:27:23.528 --> 00:27:27.260
is called a product we
have called Cloud Datalab.

00:27:27.260 --> 00:27:30.240
Now, there's a lot of key
benefits to Cloud Datalab.

00:27:30.240 --> 00:27:32.120
It's basically data
science for developers.

