Where Looker kind of
approaches this problem

00:38:50.380 --> 00:38:54.430
is where lots of other
BI tools in the past

00:38:54.430 --> 00:38:59.020
have been built around data
pipelines and databases that

00:38:59.020 --> 00:39:02.800
were row stores, or even
columnar stores that were

00:39:02.800 --> 00:39:04.980
a little bit less performant.

00:39:04.980 --> 00:39:06.880
When these services
like BigQuery

00:39:06.880 --> 00:39:10.150
came out that can separate
compute and storage--

00:39:10.150 --> 00:39:12.640
you really can run
very enormous queries.

00:39:12.640 --> 00:39:15.220
You can query 20
billion rows of data

00:39:15.220 --> 00:39:17.200
and actually get
results in seconds.

00:39:17.200 --> 00:39:20.260
And so that means that you
need different BI tools

00:39:20.260 --> 00:39:21.800
to sit on top of these things.

00:39:21.800 --> 00:39:24.310
Where tools in the
past might have

00:39:24.310 --> 00:39:27.310
pulled out portions of data
or tried to aggregate data

00:39:27.310 --> 00:39:31.730
to return analytics, now
there's just no need to do that.

00:39:31.730 --> 00:39:33.580
Looker really
approaches this problem

00:39:33.580 --> 00:39:36.490
from completely blowing up
the way that BI was done

00:39:36.490 --> 00:39:39.040
and live connecting
to all of your data.

00:39:39.040 --> 00:39:41.110
Do all this great
pipelining with Google,

00:39:41.110 --> 00:39:43.490
and we can sit
right on top of it.

00:39:43.490 --> 00:39:45.490
The next thing that you
should kind of take away

00:39:45.490 --> 00:39:48.160
from all of this is there's
a lot of different ways

00:39:48.160 --> 00:39:50.510
to do analytics on
top of your data.

00:39:50.510 --> 00:39:52.170
There are data
scientists that want

00:39:52.170 --> 00:39:55.210
to write raw SQL, and
script, and do machine

00:39:55.210 --> 00:39:56.800
learning on top of things.

00:39:56.800 --> 00:39:59.140
And they are very
non-technical people

00:39:59.140 --> 00:40:02.770
that want to visualize data,
pull reporting, ask questions

00:40:02.770 --> 00:40:06.910
of data without having
to understand SQL.

00:40:06.910 --> 00:40:08.620
As you build your
tooling, you really

00:40:08.620 --> 00:40:11.980
do want to think about balancing
all of those different users,

00:40:11.980 --> 00:40:13.910
and we're trying to
strike a balance with all

00:40:13.910 --> 00:40:14.868
those different things.

00:40:14.868 --> 00:40:17.260
So a couple key
takeaways that I would

00:40:17.260 --> 00:40:19.570
love you to remember
about Looker and just

00:40:19.570 --> 00:40:21.050
about how we work with things.

00:40:21.050 --> 00:40:23.560
One is that we're deeply
integrated with BigQuery

00:40:23.560 --> 00:40:24.550
and the Google Cloud.

00:40:24.550 --> 00:40:28.390
So you see things
like cost estimators,

00:40:28.390 --> 00:40:31.039
and size estimators of queries--

00:40:31.039 --> 00:40:32.830
we deeply integrate
those into the product.

00:40:32.830 --> 00:40:35.140
Because again, when
you're building a new way

00:40:35.140 --> 00:40:37.330
to do analytics on
top of these things

00:40:37.330 --> 00:40:39.760
where another tool
might live connect--

00:40:39.760 --> 00:40:43.990
if you can't get cost estimation
or these super features

00:40:43.990 --> 00:40:46.672
like Nested Data Structures
and things like that,

00:40:46.672 --> 00:40:48.880
you can't take advantage of
all the interesting stuff

00:40:48.880 --> 00:40:50.830
in the database.

00:40:50.830 --> 00:40:53.920
Secondarily, we have deep
support for everything

00:40:53.920 --> 00:40:55.360
that Google is doing.

00:40:55.360 --> 00:40:57.040
Google released
Spanner two weeks ago,

00:40:57.040 --> 00:40:59.380
and that's already
supported in Looker.

00:40:59.380 --> 00:41:01.360
As you adopt new technologies--

00:41:01.360 --> 00:41:04.240
again, because we're
dependent on these databases

00:41:04.240 --> 00:41:06.310
to build good stuff
and fast stuff,

00:41:06.310 --> 00:41:07.880
we're adopting them
just as quickly.

00:41:07.880 --> 00:41:09.671
So that's something
that's really important

00:41:09.671 --> 00:41:12.520
to think about as you
build your analytics stack.

00:41:12.520 --> 00:41:15.910
Second, the real
power of Looker is

00:41:15.910 --> 00:41:17.590
we're writing raw
SQL to the database,

00:41:17.590 --> 00:41:19.630
so we're taking
advantage of BigQuery.

00:41:19.630 --> 00:41:21.640
But all of this is
mediated through a model

00:41:21.640 --> 00:41:24.280
that's holding all of your
centralized definition.

00:41:24.280 --> 00:41:27.030
For people that want to write
SQL, there's all of that power.

00:41:27.030 --> 00:41:30.800
You can get at the raw
data, you can sculpt queries

00:41:30.800 --> 00:41:32.224
in a very custom way.

00:41:32.224 --> 00:41:34.390
For people that want to
point and click and use kind

00:41:34.390 --> 00:41:37.197
of a Tableau style interface
that might not ever

00:41:37.197 --> 00:41:39.280
want to interact with SQL,
we've got that as well.

00:41:39.280 --> 00:41:40.450
And that's all stored
in the data model,

00:41:40.450 --> 00:41:42.070
and we'll show you
that in a second.

00:41:42.070 --> 00:41:43.990
And then lastly,
because we're sitting

00:41:43.990 --> 00:41:48.370
on top of all of the data, you
can do rollups, and high level

00:41:48.370 --> 00:41:50.770
metrics like daily active
users and things like that,

00:41:50.770 --> 00:41:54.310
and you can get
down to row level.

00:41:54.310 --> 00:41:56.230
Before I jump into the
demo, the last thing

00:41:56.230 --> 00:41:58.810
is the power of
this data model lets

00:41:58.810 --> 00:42:01.330
us bring customized
understanding

00:42:01.330 --> 00:42:02.990
to different types of datasets.

00:42:02.990 --> 00:42:06.580
So this is about gaming
analytics, and at some level

00:42:06.580 --> 00:42:08.260
all the things we've
done so far are

00:42:08.260 --> 00:42:11.290
kind of arbitrary analytics
questions-- counting users,

00:42:11.290 --> 00:42:12.400
doing things.

00:42:12.400 --> 00:42:17.320
The nice thing about this
model that we have in between

00:42:17.320 --> 00:42:19.990
is that we can
actually go and create

00:42:19.990 --> 00:42:22.052
understanding of different
types of businesses

00:42:22.052 --> 00:42:24.260
that are immediately
installable on top of your data.

00:42:24.260 --> 00:42:28.141
So if you're recording data
in specific types of ways,

00:42:28.141 --> 00:42:29.890
right out of the box
we can connect and do

00:42:29.890 --> 00:42:32.380
things like daily
active users, and things

00:42:32.380 --> 00:42:35.050
like sessionization, and
progression analysis,

00:42:35.050 --> 00:42:36.562
and fraud analysis.

00:42:36.562 --> 00:42:38.020
Just remember to
record everything,

00:42:38.020 --> 00:42:40.102
and then we'll get
into how you guys can

00:42:40.102 --> 00:42:41.060
take advantage of that.

00:42:41.060 --> 00:42:42.435
So if we can switch
over to demo.

00:42:47.440 --> 00:42:50.740
First to kind of just go back
to exactly what I was saying--

00:42:50.740 --> 00:42:52.270
we have essentially
a marketplace

00:42:52.270 --> 00:42:55.360
of all these different types
of analytical patterns on top

00:42:55.360 --> 00:42:56.560
of dataset.

00:42:56.560 --> 00:42:58.930
As you're recording data,
we have different ways

00:42:58.930 --> 00:43:01.660
to integrate analysis on top.

00:43:01.660 --> 00:43:04.240
We have a gaming block
for gaming analytics,

00:43:04.240 --> 00:43:06.910
and we're going to get
into that a little bit.

00:43:06.910 --> 00:43:11.050
This is Looker, a very
typical dashboarding tool

00:43:11.050 --> 00:43:11.800
as you'd expect.

00:43:11.800 --> 00:43:13.970
We've built a top a
bunch of dashboards

00:43:13.970 --> 00:43:18.927
on top of that same gaming
set that we were playing with.

00:43:18.927 --> 00:43:21.010
Let's go take a look at
what these types of things

00:43:21.010 --> 00:43:22.600
look like.

00:43:22.600 --> 00:43:25.120
These are literally
just running queries

00:43:25.120 --> 00:43:26.710
to that same BigQuery dataset.

00:43:26.710 --> 00:43:29.050
So behind each of
these individual tiles

00:43:29.050 --> 00:43:31.960
is a query in BigQuery.

00:43:31.960 --> 00:43:37.000
This 52.61% completion
rate that we talked about,

00:43:37.000 --> 00:43:39.240
we can go look at the
distribution of these things--

00:43:39.240 --> 00:43:41.077
if the internet will come back.

00:43:41.077 --> 00:43:42.910
For example, we can
look at the user account

00:43:42.910 --> 00:43:44.230
at each quest level,
and we can see

00:43:44.230 --> 00:43:46.270
that there's a lot of
people stuck at level 10.

00:43:46.270 --> 00:43:49.180
So if I'm a gaming company,
this is really valuable for me

00:43:49.180 --> 00:43:51.190
to understand.

00:43:51.190 --> 00:43:53.770
As you can see this is a
fairly smooth distribution,

00:43:53.770 --> 00:43:56.590
and that's what you get
with simulated data.

00:43:56.590 --> 00:44:00.010
But what the beauty of this
model on top of this dataset

00:44:00.010 --> 00:44:04.420
lets us do is we can
actually go and explore

00:44:04.420 --> 00:44:06.320
this data more deeply-- sorry.

00:44:11.280 --> 00:44:12.272
Frozen here.

00:44:16.760 --> 00:44:19.770
Let's go look at
this gaming dataset,

00:44:19.770 --> 00:44:21.150
and we'll go look at events.

00:44:24.370 --> 00:44:27.000
So again, we're sitting on
top of the raw dataset here.

00:44:27.000 --> 00:44:34.890
So as I add fields, we're
going to actually be

00:44:34.890 --> 00:44:38.340
running SQL behind the scenes.

00:44:38.340 --> 00:44:40.440
For some people, they
might be able to read

00:44:40.440 --> 00:44:41.454
this and understand it.

00:44:41.454 --> 00:44:42.870
For some of you
it doesn't matter,

00:44:42.870 --> 00:44:44.940
because all we're going
to do is write queries.

00:44:44.940 --> 00:44:47.017
But again, the power
here is that we're

00:44:47.017 --> 00:44:48.600
taking advantage of
all the good stuff

00:44:48.600 --> 00:44:50.225
that Google is doing
behind the scenes.

00:44:50.225 --> 00:44:52.560
So you'll note that
we're going to process

00:44:52.560 --> 00:44:54.480
36 gigs for this
query, and this is just

00:44:54.480 --> 00:44:56.370
going to run in BigQuery.

00:44:56.370 --> 00:44:58.620
And this is actually
running live in database.

00:44:58.620 --> 00:45:00.960
So as latent as
your pipeline is--

00:45:00.960 --> 00:45:03.600
they talked about a
one second pipeline--

00:45:03.600 --> 00:45:09.071
we can hook up to that data and
actually query it in real-time.

00:45:09.071 --> 00:45:09.570
Sorry.

00:45:12.850 --> 00:45:15.790
So this took 13 seconds to
query those 36 gigs of data,

00:45:15.790 --> 00:45:17.440
and we've got a
bunch of raw data.

00:45:17.440 --> 00:45:19.990
What that lets us do
is actually explore

00:45:19.990 --> 00:45:22.820
this type of data in real-time.

00:45:22.820 --> 00:45:24.944
Let's go browse for some
stuff I made already.

00:45:24.944 --> 00:45:26.860
But let's look at what's
happening on level 10

00:45:26.860 --> 00:45:30.100
