but that just offloaded it
to another Google App Engine

00:50:42.000 --> 00:50:42.750
instance.

00:50:42.750 --> 00:50:46.060
So basically it was all
App Engine at the start.

00:50:46.060 --> 00:50:48.700
Then we evolved into--

00:50:48.700 --> 00:50:51.000
we started using this pipeline.

00:50:51.000 --> 00:50:55.290
Actually this is a standard
Google gaming reference

00:50:55.290 --> 00:50:57.659
architecture.

00:50:57.659 --> 00:50:58.200
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:50:58.200 --> 00:51:00.019
Thanks.

00:51:00.019 --> 00:51:01.810
AUDIENCE: I kind of
had a similar question,

00:51:01.810 --> 00:51:03.393
but to drill more
into it in the sense

00:51:03.393 --> 00:51:06.160
that you had the Task Queues
for your apps for a game engine,

00:51:06.160 --> 00:51:10.240
and you had the API specifically
for client requests feeding

00:51:10.240 --> 00:51:10.780
the Pub/Sub.

00:51:10.780 --> 00:51:13.984
And I was wondering
is there or was there

00:51:13.984 --> 00:51:16.150
the potential for a unified
approach of just writing

00:51:16.150 --> 00:51:18.400
directly to Pub/Sub and doing
all your transformation,

00:51:18.400 --> 00:51:20.140
and your validation,
and data flow?

00:51:20.140 --> 00:51:22.189
Other than the issues of--

00:51:22.189 --> 00:51:24.730
obviously you're getting some
efficiencies in micro batching,

00:51:24.730 --> 00:51:26.350
and you didn't want to handle
security on the client-- other

00:51:26.350 --> 00:51:28.000
than those two issues.

00:51:28.000 --> 00:51:29.920
MITJA BEZENSEK:
Very good question.

00:51:29.920 --> 00:51:34.780
We considered pushing all to
Pub/Sub, but at the moment

00:51:34.780 --> 00:51:37.030
we're using cap engine standard.

00:51:37.030 --> 00:51:41.980
And for Pub/Sub, there is only
the REST API client available,

00:51:41.980 --> 00:51:46.870
which has a higher latency than
the GRPC client, for example.

00:51:46.870 --> 00:51:49.870
So task queues are
really fast, so that's

00:51:49.870 --> 00:51:56.045
why we still prefer to use
Task Queues for that example.

00:51:56.045 --> 00:52:00.220
But yeah, we had to
write our own pool cue

00:52:00.220 --> 00:52:05.580
source for Dataflow,
because there's no--

00:52:05.580 --> 00:52:08.020
AUDIENCE: Or also even
replacing the API endpoint

00:52:08.020 --> 00:52:11.329
and going direct to
Pub/Sub there too.

00:52:11.329 --> 00:52:12.370
MITJA BEZENSEK: The API--

00:52:12.370 --> 00:52:13.480
AUDIENCE: The client API--

00:52:13.480 --> 00:52:15.010
the REST.

00:52:15.010 --> 00:52:16.420
The container service one.

00:52:16.420 --> 00:52:19.560
MITJA BEZENSEK: The
container service is just

00:52:19.560 --> 00:52:22.630
for ingesting the client event.

00:52:22.630 --> 00:52:27.390
So the server events are
generated by the Google App

00:52:27.390 --> 00:52:30.070
Engine back-end and
pushed to Pub/Sub.

00:52:30.070 --> 00:52:31.870
JOSEPH HOLLEY: About
having the API point,

00:52:31.870 --> 00:52:33.940
you can eliminate
that if you want,

00:52:33.940 --> 00:52:38.260
but almost every customer that
I've talked to chooses not to.

00:52:38.260 --> 00:52:41.260
One, because you can usually
keep the costs pretty

00:52:41.260 --> 00:52:42.220
minimal on that.

00:52:42.220 --> 00:52:45.460
And two, because it
gives them a place

00:52:45.460 --> 00:52:47.710
to ingest where they
can do basic validation

00:52:47.710 --> 00:52:51.430
and make sure that people aren't
sending events from non-client,

00:52:51.430 --> 00:52:54.130
and spamming your
analytics pipeline

00:52:54.130 --> 00:52:55.090
or something like that.

00:52:55.090 --> 00:52:56.560
It allows you to do a lot
of that sort of stuff.

00:52:56.560 --> 00:52:57.320
AUDIENCE: But you could
do that in Dataflow,

00:52:57.320 --> 00:52:58.240
as trying to get rid of--

00:52:58.240 --> 00:52:59.531
JOSEPH HOLLEY: Yeah, you could.

00:52:59.531 --> 00:53:03.250
AUDIENCE: Everyone is building
that custom API endpoint now.

00:53:03.250 --> 00:53:04.846
And I understand the
core issue of not

00:53:04.846 --> 00:53:06.970
wanting to manage security
on the client of running

00:53:06.970 --> 00:53:09.864
the Pub/Sub, too, and
potential micro batching.

00:53:09.864 --> 00:53:12.280
JOSEPH HOLLEY: There's some
advantages to eliminating them

00:53:12.280 --> 00:53:14.200
earlier in the
pipeline, so that they

00:53:14.200 --> 00:53:17.110
can't continue down and
generate more resource

00:53:17.110 --> 00:53:19.194
usage in further products
past the ingested point.

00:53:19.194 --> 00:53:21.651
AUDIENCE: Might you just make
that a service at some point?

00:53:21.651 --> 00:53:22.540
Like a pre-Pub/Sub--

00:53:22.540 --> 00:53:23.873
JOSEPH HOLLEY: I like your idea.

00:53:23.873 --> 00:53:25.210
Come talk to us.

00:53:25.210 --> 00:53:28.120
MITJA BEZENSEK: I mean, I
understand your question now.

00:53:28.120 --> 00:53:30.880
Sorry, I really
didn't get it before.

00:53:30.880 --> 00:53:34.330
But the main point
is security for me.

00:53:34.330 --> 00:53:37.480
I wouldn't allow
our remote clients--

00:53:37.480 --> 00:53:41.260
I wouldn't give our remote
clients our Google service

00:53:41.260 --> 00:53:42.384
account to use--

00:53:42.384 --> 00:53:43.050
AUDIENCE: Yeah--

00:53:43.050 --> 00:53:43.720
MITJA BEZENSEK:
--directly Pub/Sub.

00:53:43.720 --> 00:53:44.210
AUDIENCE: --it's still messy.

00:53:44.210 --> 00:53:44.709
Yeah.

00:53:44.709 --> 00:53:46.179
Thanks.

00:53:46.179 --> 00:53:48.220
DANIEL GRACHANIN: On that
question, too, tomorrow

00:53:48.220 --> 00:53:49.450
we can talk more after
we do some more product

00:53:49.450 --> 00:53:50.033
announcements.

00:53:50.033 --> 00:53:52.460
We might be able to
better address that.

00:53:52.460 --> 00:53:53.710
JOSEPH HOLLEY: Go ahead.

00:53:53.710 --> 00:53:55.480
AUDIENCE: I have a
