00:54:38.466 --> 00:54:40.090
AUDIENCE: I just
wanted to give a sense

00:54:40.090 --> 00:54:42.190
for the cost of some of
those BigQuery queries,

00:54:42.190 --> 00:54:43.360
like maybe the larger ones.

00:54:43.360 --> 00:54:46.399
Are we talking cents,
dollars, tens of dollars?

00:54:46.399 --> 00:54:48.940
DANIEL GRACHANIN: So BigQuery
only charges for what you store

00:54:48.940 --> 00:54:51.040
and what you actually compute.

00:54:51.040 --> 00:54:52.745
It's hard to say for
each one of them.

00:54:52.745 --> 00:54:54.120
AUDIENCE: So when
we query stuff,

00:54:54.120 --> 00:54:56.270
I think the cost
comes up every time.

00:54:56.270 --> 00:54:58.436
Just curious, roughly what
are those larger queries?

00:54:58.436 --> 00:55:00.285
COLIN ZIMA: I believe
it's $5 a terabyte.

00:55:00.285 --> 00:55:01.160
MITJA BEZENSEK: Yeah.

00:55:01.160 --> 00:55:01.514
DANIEL GRACHANIN: Oh, I'm sorry.

00:55:01.514 --> 00:55:02.830
I thought you meant
those specific queries.

00:55:02.830 --> 00:55:04.413
AUDIENCE: The queries
you were making,

00:55:04.413 --> 00:55:06.710
were they cents, dollars,
hundreds of dollars?

00:55:06.710 --> 00:55:10.415
COLIN ZIMA: $5 a terabyte
means that 30 gigs is $0.20.

00:55:10.415 --> 00:55:11.290
AUDIENCE: I get that.

00:55:11.290 --> 00:55:13.290
JOSEPH HOLLEY: Yeah,
that's a really good point.

00:55:13.290 --> 00:55:15.070
If any of you use that
UI, you're actually

00:55:15.070 --> 00:55:17.278
going to get a little cost
estimator of how much it's

00:55:17.278 --> 00:55:20.650
going to cost based on the
amount of data it's querying.

00:55:20.650 --> 00:55:22.270
We were logged into
a Google account

00:55:22.270 --> 00:55:24.497
and it hides that
for us by default,

00:55:24.497 --> 00:55:26.080
and we didn't turn
