00:55:26.080 --> 00:55:26.920
That's all that is.

00:55:26.920 --> 00:55:28.128
AUDIENCE: Other question is--

00:55:28.128 --> 00:55:30.340
I didn't see Bigtable
mentioned anywhere.

00:55:30.340 --> 00:55:32.167
Was that in your consideration?

00:55:32.167 --> 00:55:34.000
DANIEL GRACHANIN: There's
case for Bigtable.

00:55:34.000 --> 00:55:35.486
More importantly now Spanner--

00:55:35.486 --> 00:55:36.610
that's global availability.

00:55:36.610 --> 00:55:38.050
There's a lot of cases
that we didn't mention

00:55:38.050 --> 00:55:39.160
within this particular pipe.

00:55:39.160 --> 00:55:40.576
And something about
BigQuery, too,

00:55:40.576 --> 00:55:43.970
is we have quite a few cost
control mechanisms in our ACLs,

00:55:43.970 --> 00:55:44.470
too.

00:55:44.470 --> 00:55:46.330
We can get very fine
grained as far as calling

00:55:46.330 --> 00:55:48.580
if you can or can't do a
join or something like that.

00:55:48.580 --> 00:55:49.996
So if you're worried
about someone

00:55:49.996 --> 00:55:53.050
on your team, data scientists
running up your bill,

00:55:53.050 --> 00:55:55.752
you can put those cost
controls in place.

00:55:55.752 --> 00:55:56.710
So that's helpful, too.

00:55:56.710 --> 00:55:59.574
And we also have packaging
too with BigQuery for--

00:55:59.574 --> 00:56:01.990
if you run a lot of queries,
you can do a certain package,

00:56:01.990 --> 00:56:03.760
or you can do it with a
typical model we have too.

00:56:03.760 --> 00:56:05.926
JOSEPH HOLLEY: We do have
a couple of game customers

00:56:05.926 --> 00:56:08.710
who do run analytics
load on BigQuery.

00:56:08.710 --> 00:56:11.620
And mostly it has to
do with specific things

00:56:11.620 --> 00:56:13.420
that they are trying to do.

00:56:13.420 --> 00:56:15.610
For the vast majority
of customers,

00:56:15.610 --> 00:56:19.150
we recommend BigQuery
over Bigtable

00:56:19.150 --> 00:56:22.150
mostly just because it takes
care of all the management

00:56:22.150 --> 00:56:22.900
part for you.

00:56:22.900 --> 00:56:26.020
Whereas Bigtable does
actually have nodes

00:56:26.020 --> 00:56:31.060
that you need to add in order
to meet higher IOPS thresholds,

00:56:31.060 --> 00:56:33.040
and being able to query
it, or insert more data

00:56:33.040 --> 00:56:34.614
into it that
requires more nodes.

00:56:34.614 --> 00:56:36.280
So there's a management
component there.

00:56:36.280 --> 00:56:37.696
If you're willing
to take that on,

00:56:37.696 --> 00:56:39.839
it can be a really good
analytic solution as well.

00:56:39.839 --> 00:56:40.380
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:56:40.380 --> 00:56:40.580
Thanks.

00:56:40.580 --> 00:56:41.440
DANIEL GRACHANIN: It
also depends on use case,

00:56:41.440 --> 00:56:43.184
because Bigtable's
read and write,

00:56:43.184 --> 00:56:45.100
and BigQuery does not
have that functionality.

00:56:45.100 --> 00:56:47.422
So for Bigtable-- yeah.

00:56:47.422 --> 00:56:49.877
AUDIENCE: I have a
