00:59:59.620 --> 01:00:00.250
Quick question.

01:00:00.250 --> 01:00:02.290
So let's say you're
using BigQuery

01:00:02.290 --> 01:00:06.550
to even record events like
sales, in-app purchases, right?

01:00:06.550 --> 01:00:09.640
Have you had any issues
with de-duplication

01:00:09.640 --> 01:00:12.880
of events going into the query?

01:00:12.880 --> 01:00:14.540
MITJA BEZENSEK: Excuse
me, replication?

01:00:14.540 --> 01:00:14.876
COLIN ZIMA: De-duplication.

01:00:14.876 --> 01:00:15.917
AUDIENCE: De-duplication.

01:00:15.917 --> 01:00:17.560
MITJA BEZENSEK:
Oh, de-duplication.

01:00:17.560 --> 01:00:22.390
De-duplication, part of it is
carried by the Dataflow job,

01:00:22.390 --> 01:00:24.460
by the Pub/Sub ingestion.

01:00:24.460 --> 01:00:27.970
So the Pub/Sub
source has a support

01:00:27.970 --> 01:00:29.770
to de-duplicate records.

01:00:29.770 --> 01:00:32.020
And also when you
write BigQuery--

01:00:32.020 --> 01:00:36.700
the BigQuery streamer that's
used in Google Cloud Dataflow--

01:00:36.700 --> 01:00:39.580
has also de-duplication
on its own.

01:00:39.580 --> 01:00:42.640
It's not that good as
on the Pub/Sub source,

01:00:42.640 --> 01:00:47.080
but it still gets a
lot of de-dub done.

01:00:47.080 --> 01:00:51.760
In general, Google takes
care with their SDK

01:00:51.760 --> 01:00:53.587
or APIs for that.

01:00:53.587 --> 01:00:55.420
COLIN ZIMA: I'd add
that analytics tools can

01:00:55.420 --> 01:00:56.710
add a third layer to that.

01:00:56.710 --> 01:00:59.590
So analytics tools can do some
of that de-duplication as well.

01:00:59.590 --> 01:01:00.020
AUDIENCE: All right.

01:01:00.020 --> 01:01:00.850
DANIEL GRACHANIN: Pub/Sub
will take care of most it.

01:01:00.850 --> 01:01:01.273
Yeah.

01:01:01.273 --> 01:01:02.606
JOSEPH HOLLEY: Thanks, everyone.

01:01:02.606 --> 01:01:03.920
COLIN ZIMA: Thanks.

01:01:03.920 --> 01:01:06.070
[MUSIC PLAYING]

